\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Site factors after summation over links}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For the \vocabk{orbit gauge–Higgs weight}{fdip-010A} over a
commutative unital ring #{k}, the sum of #{\Xi(U,\phi)} over
#{U} is a single coefficient #{w_*\in k} independent of #{\phi}.
Multiply the joint weight by arbitrary functions
#{t_x:\mathcal O\to k}, one at each site, evaluated at #{\phi_x}.
Write #{z_x=\sum_{o\in\mathcal O}t_x(o)}. The resulting scalar
marginal and partition coefficient are
##{w_{{\rm site},t}(\phi)=w_*\prod_x t_x(\phi_x),
 \qquad Z_t=w_*\prod_x z_x.}
For #{o\in\mathcal O} the unnormalized weight of #{\phi_x=o} is
##{w_*t_x(o)\prod_{y\ne x}z_y.}
If #{Z_t} is a unit, the normalized scalar weight is the product
of the one-site weights #{t_x(\phi_x)/z_x}. In particular a
#{k}-module-valued test function #{f} of the selected site has value
##{\langle f(\phi_x)\rangle_t=z_x^{-1}\sum_{o\in\mathcal O}t_x(o)f(o).}}

\proof{
\p{For two scalar configurations choose a group element at each site
carrying its first value to its second, using transitivity of the
orbit. Together these elements form a vertex gauge. The
[scalar marginal weight](fdip-010E) is invariant by \ref{fdip-010G},
so both configurations give the same sum over links. The extra
factors are independent of the links and multiply this sum.
Summation in each scalar variable gives #{z_y}; holding the
selected value fixed omits precisely that site's sum.}

\p{In a commutative ring each factor of a unit product is a unit.
Thus the joint normalization supplies inverses of #{w_*} and of
every #{z_y}. Dividing the two unnormalized identities by #{Z_t}
now gives the product and its single-site functional. If only
#{z_x} is a unit, the one-orbit quotient still defines a functional
on that orbit. A joint normalized value requires #{Z_t} itself
to be a unit.}
}
